::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes a claim? {
=A statement that someone says is true#Correct. A claim is a statement that needs support.
~A mark that shows equal lengths#This describes possible evidence, not a claim.
~A drawing that replaces reasoning#A drawing can help, but it does not replace reasoning.
~A mistake that cannot be corrected#A claim can be supported, revised, or rejected based on evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes a conjecture? {
~A conclusion that never needs support#A conjecture needs testing and support.
~A diagram marking that always means parallel#A conjecture is a type of claim, not a specific diagram marking.
=A claim based on observations or patterns that still needs support#Correct. A conjecture is suggested by observations but should be tested.
~A final proof written with no evidence#A conjecture is not a final proof and still needs support.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes evidence? {
=Information that supports a claim or conclusion#Correct. Evidence is used to support mathematical reasoning.
~A guess based on appearance#A guess is not strong mathematical evidence.
~A claim with no reason#A claim needs evidence; it is not evidence by itself.
~A conclusion that ignores the diagram#Ignoring the diagram does not provide support.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes a mathematical argument? {
~A list of answers without reasons#A list of answers does not explain why a conclusion is supported.
=A set of statements that explains why a conclusion is supported#Correct. A mathematical argument connects claims, evidence, and reasoning.
~A drawing that looks convincing#Appearance alone is not a valid argument.
~A conjecture that is accepted without testing#A conjecture still needs support before it is accepted.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes flawed reasoning? {
~Reasoning that uses relevant evidence#Relevant evidence strengthens reasoning instead of making it flawed.
~Reasoning that clearly connects evidence to a claim#This describes a valid argument.
~Reasoning that uses definitions accurately#Accurate definitions support strong reasoning.
=Reasoning that is incomplete, unsupported, or based on an incorrect idea#Correct. Flawed reasoning does not adequately support the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows segment AB and segment CD with matching tick marks. Which claim is supported? {
~AB is longer than CD.#Matching tick marks show equal length, not one segment being longer.
~AB is perpendicular to CD.#Tick marks do not show perpendicular relationships.
=AB = CD#Correct. Matching tick marks support the claim that the segment lengths are equal.
~A, B, C, and D are collinear.#Tick marks do not show that all four points lie on the same line.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows angle A and angle B with matching arc marks. Which conclusion is supported? {
~Angle A is a right angle.#Matching arc marks show congruence, not necessarily a 90&deg; measure.
=m&angle;A = m&angle;B#Correct. Matching arc marks support the conclusion that the angles have equal measure.
~Angle A is larger than angle B.#Matching arc marks show equal measures, not larger or smaller measures.
~The angles are supplementary.#Supplementary angles require evidence that their measures add to 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows a square corner mark at angle RST. Which argument is valid? {
=Angle RST measures 90&deg; because a square corner mark shows a right angle.#Correct. The evidence supports the conclusion.
~Angle RST is 45&deg; because it looks half of a right angle.#Appearance is not enough, and the square corner mark shows a right angle.
~Angle RST is congruent to every angle in the diagram.#The square corner mark only identifies angle RST as a right angle.
~Angle RST is a segment because it has a corner.#An angle is not a segment, and the square mark gives angle information.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which argument is valid? {
~The points are collinear because they are close together.#Closeness does not prove collinearity.
~The points are collinear because they have similar names.#Names do not prove that points lie on the same line.
=The points are collinear because they lie on the same straight line.#Correct. This uses the definition of collinear points as evidence.
~The points are collinear because the diagram looks neat.#Appearance and neatness are not evidence for collinearity.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which argument is flawed? {
~The segment lengths are equal because matching tick marks are shown.#This is supported by relevant diagram evidence.
~The angle is right because a square corner mark is shown.#This is supported by a right angle marking.
~The points are collinear because they lie on the same straight line.#This uses the definition of collinear points.
=The lines are parallel because they look parallel.#Correct. Appearance alone is not enough evidence for parallel lines.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says two segments are congruent because they are both horizontal. What is the flaw? {
=The evidence is not relevant to the claim about length.#Correct. Segment direction does not support a congruence conclusion.
~Horizontal direction proves equal length.#This repeats the misconception. Direction does not determine length.
~The student used tick marks correctly.#No tick marks are mentioned; the student used direction.
~The student proved the segments are perpendicular.#The claim is about congruence, not perpendicular lines.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says two angles are congruent because they look equal. What evidence would better support the claim? {
~The angles are drawn near each other.#Location does not support angle congruence.
~The angles are drawn with the same pencil.#Drawing style does not support mathematical congruence.
~The angles are part of the same worksheet.#Being on the same worksheet is not geometric evidence.
=The angles have matching arc marks or equal measures.#Correct. Matching arc marks or equal measures directly support angle congruence.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says one example proves a conjecture. What is the best response? {
~One example always proves a conjecture.#One example can suggest a conjecture, but it is not enough for a general conclusion.
=One example can suggest a conjecture, but more support is needed.#Correct. A stronger argument needs more evidence or reasoning.
~The conjecture must be false.#One example does not prove the conjecture false.
~Examples cannot help form conjectures.#Examples can help form conjectures, but they must be supported.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which sentence best connects evidence to a claim? {
~The answer is right because I know it.#This does not give evidence.
~The lines look parallel, so they are parallel.#This relies on appearance, not strong evidence.
=The segments are congruent because they have matching tick marks.#Correct. This connects the claim to relevant evidence.
~The angles are equal because the picture is nice.#The appearance of the picture is not mathematical evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation is incomplete? {
=The segments are congruent.#Correct. This is only a claim and does not provide evidence.
~The angle is right because it has a square corner mark.#This gives evidence and reasoning.
~The points are collinear because they lie on the same straight line.#This connects a definition to evidence.
~The angles are congruent because their measures are both 50&deg;.#This uses equal measures as evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What should come after a claim in a strong mathematical argument? {
~A guess about what looks true#A guess does not support the claim.
=Relevant evidence and reasoning#Correct. Evidence and reasoning explain why the claim is supported.
~A different unrelated claim#An unrelated claim does not support the original claim.
~No explanation at all#A claim without explanation is incomplete.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is a conjecture rather than a supported conclusion? {
~Segment AB equals segment CD because matching tick marks are shown.#This is supported by diagram evidence.
~Angle A measures 90&deg; because it has a square corner mark.#This is supported by a right angle marking.
=I think this pattern will continue because it worked in the first few examples.#Correct. This is a conjecture based on observed examples and still needs support.
~Points A, B, and C are collinear because they lie on the same line.#This conclusion is supported by the definition of collinear points.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which item is relevant evidence for the claim "the angles are congruent"? {
~The angles are different colors.#Color is not evidence of equal angle measure.
~The angles are drawn close together.#Location does not prove congruence.
~The angles are labeled with different letters.#Labels identify angles but do not prove equal measure.
=The angles have the same measure.#Correct. Equal measures support the claim that angles are congruent.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which item is relevant evidence for the claim "the segments are congruent"? {
~The segments are both drawn diagonally.#Direction does not prove equal length.
=The segments have matching tick marks.#Correct. Matching tick marks support segment congruence.
~The segments are near each other.#Location does not prove congruence.
~The segments use letters from the alphabet.#Labels identify objects but do not prove equal length.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which item is relevant evidence for the claim "the lines are parallel"? {
=The lines have parallel markings or are stated to be parallel.#Correct. Markings or a given statement support a parallel-line claim.
~The lines are drawn in the same color.#Color does not prove lines are parallel.
~The lines look like they go the same direction.#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
~The lines are on the same page.#Being on the same page is not geometric evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best revises the flawed argument "The segments are equal because they look equal"? {
~The segments are equal because I said so.#This still gives no mathematical evidence.
~The segments are equal because they are both drawn in black.#Color does not prove length.
~The segments are equal because they are on the same diagram.#Being on the same diagram is not evidence of equal length.
=The segments are equal because matching tick marks show congruent segments.#Correct. This revision uses relevant evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best revises the flawed argument "The angle is 90&deg; because it looks square"? {
~The angle is 90&deg; because it is near another angle.#Location does not support a right angle conclusion.
~The angle is 90&deg; because it is drawn carefully.#Careful drawing is not enough evidence.
=The angle is 90&deg; because it has a square corner mark.#Correct. A square corner mark supports a right angle conclusion.
~The angle is 90&deg; because all angles are 90&deg;.#Not all angles are right angles.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best revises the flawed argument "The points are on one line because they look lined up"? {
=The points are collinear because they are shown on the same straight line.#Correct. This connects the claim to relevant diagram evidence.
~The points are on one line because they are dots.#Being dots does not show collinearity.
~The points are on one line because they have capital letters.#Labels identify points but do not prove collinearity.
~The points are on one line because they are close together.#Closeness does not prove points are collinear.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes why appearance alone is not proof? {
~Diagrams are never useful.#Diagrams are useful, but they must be read with labels, markings, and given information.
=A drawing may look a certain way without providing enough evidence for the conclusion.#Correct. Appearance can suggest an idea, but evidence is needed.
~Only numbers can be evidence.#Markings, definitions, and given statements can also be evidence.
~A conclusion is always true if it looks true.#This is the misconception; looking true is not enough proof.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the main purpose of this readiness lesson? {
~To assign a formal Geometry benchmark#This lesson is readiness support only and does not introduce a new primary MLA standard.
~To memorize advanced proof formats#Formal proof formats come later; this lesson builds reasoning readiness.
~To decide conclusions by how diagrams look#The lesson teaches that appearance alone is not proof.
=To support claims and conjectures with evidence and recognize flawed reasoning#Correct. The lesson prepares students to make stronger mathematical arguments.
}

